A||||8||btoth||Dumb break design||||8||
Z||000000||btoth||02-09-2001||11:18 AM||||Why did GM design the breaks so that you have to use the parking break regularly to keep your rear calipers adjusted!?  I have an automatic and usually forget to use it and after even a day of not using it my breaks don't stop as well. I'm just glad that the Midias guy told me how they work. <P>For my technical question:  has anyone redone the rear breaks so that the calipers adjust by backing up like on most cars?  Thanks.||||reg||8
Z||000001||rogergarrison||02-09-2001||11:48 AM||||seems stupid to me too, but that what the engineers make thousands of dollars for, LOL. I dont think any type of conversion can eliminate it though. At least no ones ever said anything about it.<P>Im in Reynoldsburg, what no name city you in?||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||02-09-2001||12:00 PM||||i ditched the parking brake in my cars and i've never have any problems with the calipers. they still lock up tight enough to spin me around.. <P>86 SE 4spd - mine<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>87 GT auto - mine<BR>87 CP 4spd<P>myself and the owners needed a good spot for a fire extinguisher (my cars) a hard CD case and the other well, the p-brake sucked anyway.. so why have the extra weight.||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||02-09-2001||12:43 PM||||fire extinguisher fits perfect on the floor behind seat, dont even need to use bracket and its out of site and out of the way. I have cassettes, in front of back window makes great tape shelf, lol.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||02-09-2001||01:27 PM||||Btoth<BR>The adjusting while backing up is from a time when self adjusting drum brakes first hit the makket, doesn't apply to disks.||||reg||1
Z||000005||btoth||02-09-2001||02:06 PM||||So that only applys to drum breaks... ah.  So are other cars with rear discs the same way?  And how to the fronts stay adjusted?  Just curious.  I'm surprised that it doesn't mention anything in the owners manual about having to use the parking break regularly.  Oh well.     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><p>[This message has been edited by btoth (edited 02-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||fierospeeder||02-09-2001||02:24 PM||||Its a good idea to lubricate and use your parking brake a few times to prevent the cable from rusting.  Otherwise you have to get a new one and reroute it through the car.||||reg||1
Z||000007||GT GT 87||02-09-2001||03:50 PM||||I was never able to keep the P-brake cables from freezing up .Even new cables stoped working after a few months.I have removed them for 2 years now and rear brakes work fine. You can adjust them if needed by grabing the lever on the caliper with a large chanel lock pliers and cranking the lever back and forth.         Steve||||reg||1
Z||000008||Will||02-09-2001||10:27 PM||||The fronts don't need adjusting because they are hydraulic only, with no mechanical backup . The rears need to keep the mechanical parts adjusted, not the hydraulics.<BR>If you do the Grand-Am/Beretta brake upgrade in the rear you will get rid of your parking brake and have a hydraulic only setup in the back.||||reg||1
